  "content": {
    "id": "p71-q18-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "18",
    "prompt": "Find the last term that makes the following factorable, then factorise.\nThere are several possibilities for each.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p71-q18-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": {
          "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
          "version": 1,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "p71-q18-a-prompt",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 1,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "x^2+4x+",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "cloze",
                  "answer": "$4$",
                  "width": 13
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "For example, $x^2+4x+4=(x+2)^2$ or $x^2+4x+3=(x+1)(x+3)$.",
          "worked": "Choose $4$: the first and last terms are squares, and $2(x)(2)=4x$.\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2+4x+4&=x^2+2(x)(2)+2^2\\\\&=(x+2)^2\\end{align*}$$\nAlternatively, $1+3=4$ and $1\\times3=3$, so $x^2+4x+3=(x+1)(x+3)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 16
      },
      {
        "id": "p71-q18-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": {
          "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
          "version": 1,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "p71-q18-b-prompt",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 1,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "x^2-4x+",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "cloze",
                  "answer": "$4$",
                  "width": 13
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "For example, $x^2-4x+4=(x-2)^2$ or $x^2-4x+3=(x-1)(x-3)$.",
          "worked": "Choose $4$: the first and last terms are squares, and $-2(x)(2)=-4x$.\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2-4x+4&=x^2-2(x)(2)+2^2\\\\&=(x-2)^2\\end{align*}$$\nAlternatively, $-1+(-3)=-4$ and $(-1)(-3)=3$, so $x^2-4x+3=(x-1)(x-3)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 16
      },
      {
        "id": "p71-q18-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": {
          "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
          "version": 1,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "p71-q18-c-prompt",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 1,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "x^2-4x-",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "cloze",
                  "answer": "$12$",
                  "width": 13
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "For example, $x^2-4x-12=(x-6)(x+2)$; $x^2-4x-32=(x-8)(x+4)$; $x^2-4x-21=(x-7)(x+3)$. Infinite solutions.",
          "worked": "Choose $12$: $-6+2=-4$ and $(-6)(2)=-12$.\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2-4x-12&=x^2-6x+2x-12\\\\&=x(x-6)+2(x-6)\\\\&=(x-6)(x+2)\\end{align*}$$\nOther examples are $x^2-4x-32=(x-8)(x+4)$ and $x^2-4x-21=(x-7)(x+3)$.\nThere are infinitely many choices: for any positive integer $n$, $x^2-4x-n(n+4)=(x-(n+4))(x+n)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 22
      },
      {
        "id": "p71-q18-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": {
          "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
          "version": 1,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "p71-q18-d-prompt",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 1,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "x^2-x-",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "cloze",
                  "answer": "$6$",
                  "width": 13
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "For example, $x^2-x-6=(x-3)(x+2)$; $x^2-x-12=(x-4)(x+3)$; $x^2-x-20=(x-5)(x+4)$. Infinite solutions.",
          "worked": "Choose $6$: $-3+2=-1$ and $(-3)(2)=-6$.\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2-x-6&=x^2-3x+2x-6\\\\&=x(x-3)+2(x-3)\\\\&=(x-3)(x+2)\\end{align*}$$\nOther examples are $x^2-x-12=(x-4)(x+3)$ and $x^2-x-20=(x-5)(x+4)$.\nThere are infinitely many choices: for any positive integer $n$, $x^2-x-n(n+1)=(x-(n+1))(x+n)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 22
      },
      {
        "id": "p71-q18-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": {
          "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
          "version": 1,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "p71-q18-e-prompt",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 1,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "x^2-5x",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "cloze",
                  "answer": "$-14$",
                  "width": 13
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "For example, $x^2-5x-14=(x-7)(x+2)$; $x^2-5x+6=(x-3)(x-2)$; $x^2-5x+4=(x-4)(x-1)$.",
          "worked": "Choose $-14$: $-7+2=-5$ and $(-7)(2)=-14$.\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2-5x-14&=x^2-7x+2x-14\\\\&=x(x-7)+2(x-7)\\\\&=(x-7)(x+2)\\end{align*}$$\nPositive last terms also work: $-3+(-2)=-5$ and $(-3)(-2)=6$, giving $x^2-5x+6=(x-3)(x-2)$; $-4+(-1)=-5$ and $(-4)(-1)=4$, giving $x^2-5x+4=(x-4)(x-1)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 22
      }
    ]
  },
